p-pricing.text:
I wouldn’t add anything to Ticket at all – the day‑type is a pricing concern, not a ticket concern. If I *had* to expose it, I’d add a tiny helper:

```go
func (t Ticket) IsWeekend() bool {
    // entryTime is UTC Unix ts; weekend = Sat/Sun
    weekday := time.Unix(t.entryTime, 0).UTC().Weekday()
    return weekday == time.Saturday || weekday == time.Sunday
}
```

Then the only caller that cares is the pricing strategy. `TimeBasedPricing.Fee` (or a new WeekendTimePricing implementation) would call t.IsWeekend() to apply the multiplier. No other class needs to know; the strategy encapsulates the rule and `ParkingLot.CalculateFee` just delegates to the strategy as before.

p-refusal.text:
I’d let the caller inspect the error’s concrete type. FindAvailableSpot does something like:

```go
if err != nil {
    if r, ok := err.(RefusalError); ok {
        switch r.Reason {
        case RefusalReasonSizeMismatch:
            // tell driver “no spot big enough”
        case RefusalReasonSpotUnavailable:
            // tell driver “garage full”
        }
    }
    return Spot{}, err
}
```

Returning a bool would lose that granularity – you’d have to add another out‑parameter or make callers re‑run the allocation just to know why it failed. An explicit error with a domain‑specific enum keeps the decision point in one place and makes the UI/message mapping trivial.
